<BaseLayout {title} {description} lang="en"><section class="case-intro"><span class="case-no">ARCHITECTURE STORY / 04</span><h1>Modernize without erasing the business.</h1><p class="lede">Enterprise modernization is not a rewrite contest. It is deciding what must change, what must remain stable and where new boundaries make future evolution cheaper.</p></section><div class="case-facts"><div><small>Context</small><strong>Long-lived enterprise systems</strong></div><div><small>Evolution</small><strong>Java EE → Spring Boot</strong></div><div><small>Focus</small><strong>APIs · Integration · Maintainability</strong></div></div><section class="case-body"><div class="prose"><h2>Legacy contains knowledge.</h2><div><p>Long-lived retail and logistics applications encode years of business rules and operational dependencies. Modernization therefore cannot start by assuming that old means disposable.</p><p>My work has included evolving Java EE applications toward Spring Boot, introducing REST APIs and clearer integration boundaries while preserving the operational knowledge embedded in existing systems.</p></div></div><div class="system-flow"><span class="node">Legacy flows</span><span class="link">→</span><span class="node accent">Explicit boundaries</span><span class="link">→</span><span class="node">REST APIs</span><span class="link">→</span><span class="node">Evolvable services</span></div><div class="decision-list"><div class="decision"><b>01</b><strong>Preserve business behavior</strong><p>Understand existing rules and dependencies before changing the implementation.</p></div><div class="decision"><b>02</b><strong>Create boundaries</strong><p>Use APIs and clearer responsibilities to reduce coupling around evolving capabilities.</p></div><div class="decision"><b>03</b><strong>Modernize incrementally</strong><p>Move technology forward without making operational continuity depend on a big-bang rewrite.</p></div></div></section></BaseLayout>